============================================================
SUMMARY: Iso class counts by mapping
============================================================

The MOST RESTRICTED mapping = fewest iso classes.
The MOST USEFUL mapping = captures LRC condition + few classes.

RANKINGS (fewer = better for proof):
  1. Distance-rank: n pointed classes (trivially transitive)
     BUT: runner structure lost. Walk = walk on {0,...,n-1}.
  2. Layered: O(n) classes (partition by distance shell)
     Captures coarse structure. Lonely = layer0 empty.
  3. Modular: O(A000568(sqrt(n))) classes
     CRT structure preserved. Lonely = observer beats all classes.
  4. Apex-reduced: A000568(n-2) classes (one fewer runner)
     Conditioned on apex. Captures the INTERIOR structure.
  5. Standard: A000016(n-1) classes (current best)

THE KEY INSIGHT: the distance-rank mapping collapses to n classes
because the tournament is ALWAYS TRANSITIVE (total order by distance).
This is the SIMPLEST possible mapping. The walk on {0,...,n-1}
(observer position in the distance ranking) IS the observer outdegree walk.
LRC = the walk reaches position n-1 (observer at the bottom = all safe).

But the distance-rank walk is NOT just a random walk on {0,...,n-1}.
It's constrained by the runner dynamics. The CONSTRAINTS come from
the runner sub-tournament — which the distance-rank mapping LOSES.

The IDEAL mapping preserves enough runner structure to constrain the walk
while having few enough classes for a tractable reachability analysis.
The MODULAR mapping at n=14 with modulus 7 gives O(A000568(8))=O(6880)
classes — but the CRT restriction makes most unrealizable.
The ACTUAL count of realizable CRT-modular classes at n=14 may be << 6880.

